\DOC LOCAL_JDOUBLE_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_JDOUBLE_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal one step across a called point-doubling
subroutine, replacing the current state's assumptions by those for the next
state and adding a summary assumption relating the input coordinates to the
doubled result.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index {n} of the new state variable introduced
for the state reached after the call; the assumptions and the final state
argument of the conclusion are re-expressed in terms of state {n}. The tactic
applies to a goal whose conclusion is {eventually <machine> P s}, where the
assumptions {A} contain program/register/memory {read} facts about the current
state {s} together with a {MAYCHANGE} frame assumption.
{
   A ?- eventually <machine> P s
   ========================================  LOCAL_JDOUBLE_TAC n
   A' ?- eventually <machine> P s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is the new state named by the argument, and the
postcondition {P} is unchanged apart from the state argument. In {A'}, every
assumption mentioning the old state is discharged and replaced by the
corresponding assumption about {s'}: program loaded, program counter and stack
pointer at the caller's return point, preserved register and memory contents,
and a {MAYCHANGE} assumption extended with the subroutine's additional
clobbered locations. Assumptions not mentioning the state (arithmetic side
conditions, earlier universally quantified representation facts) are kept. One
new universally quantified assumption is added, stating that for any point {z1}
represented by the input triple, the output memory triple in {s'} represents
the group doubling of {z1}.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces this is the only kind of transformation performed,
always on goals of this shape with a large assumption list describing a machine
state.

\ENDDOC
